Program:
  trait Q {}
  fun pick<T, U where U is Q, T == U>(x: T) {}
  fun f<A0, A1, A2 where A0 == A1, A1 == A2, A2 is Q>(x: A0) { pick(x) }
Results (r.sh, eq2-e965a058dfd0): baseline 0 diagnostics (94 ms); FIX 1 diagnostic, 'no coercion from '<T, U> (Q<U>, (T ~ U)) ==> [Void](let T) let -> Void' to '[Void](let A0) let -> Void''; FULL the same.
Baseline trace (legitimate derivation, no coercion step and no dropped binding): open goal A0 ~ ?u <- transitivity<A0, ?m, ?u>; ?m ~ ?u <- $w : A1 ~ A2; A0 ~ A1 <- $q; Q<A2> <- $12. Result 'resolve [Void](let A0) ...: 5 layers, 1 done, 1 minimal  penalty 6: pick<A0, A2>($12)(transitivity<A0,A1,A2>($q)($w))'.
FIX trace: '5 layers, 4 done, 4 minimal'; two results at penalty 1: 'pick<A0, A1>(coercion($12))($q)' and 'pick<A0, A2>($12)(coercion($w))', plus U := A0 at penalty 3 and penalty 5. The two penalty-1 results tie, so the call is ambiguous.
Same defect through a conditional given: o2-open-chain.hylo (given <T, U where U is Q, T == U> => Box<T> is P, need<Box<A0>>()): baseline accepts (134 ms), FIX and FULL reject. o3 and o4 behave the same (baseline 400 and 530 ms). o1 (a single link) is accepted by all three. Swapping the where-clause order to 'T == U, U is Q' (o2d-order.hylo) is accepted by FIX: FIX's acceptance depends on the order of the requirements, the baseline's does not. semtest flags o2b and x10b as MISMATCH.

Program:
  trait Collection { type Element }
  struct Box<T> { public memberwise init }
  struct Flag { public memberwise init }
  extension <T where T is Collection, T.Element == Flag> Box<T> {
    type Element = Flag
    fun first() -> Element { Flag() }
  }
  fun g<T where T is Collection, T.Element == Flag>(b: Box<T>) { let f: Flag = b.first() }
baseline: 0 diagnostics (1957 ms). FIX: 2 diagnostics, 'circular extension: the extendee of this extension depends on a lookup of 'Element', which this extension may affect' at 5.1-10 and 'type 'Box<T>' has no member 'first''. FULL: the same.
Other valid programs with the same pattern that the baseline accepts with 0 diagnostics and FIX/FULL reject with 1 'circular extension' error:
- x01-ext-arg-member.hylo: 'extension Wrapper<List.Element> { type Element = List.Element; fun first() -> Element {...} }' plus a use. semtest MISMATCH.
- x08 (the same program, declarations reordered): rejected in both orders.
- x09-ext-generic-where-assoc.hylo: 'extension <C is Collection> Wrapper<C.Element> { type Element = C.Element }'.
- x03-ext-nested-struct.hylo: 'struct List { struct Node {} }; extension List.Node { type Node = List.Node; fun next() -> Node {...} }'.
- x05-alias-ext.hylo: the same through 'type Node = List.Node; extension Node { type Node = ... }'.
- x06-nested-ext-no-alias.hylo: 'extension List.Node { fun Node() {} }'.
- x02-ext-nested.hylo: 'struct A { type Foo = B }; extension A.Foo { type Foo = A }'.

Program:
  trait P {}
  fun g<V where V is P>() {}
  fun f<A, B where B == A>() {
    fun h<C where C == A, B is P, C is P>() { g<A>() }
  }
baseline: 0 diagnostics. Its trace shows 'resolve [Void]() let -> Void: 4 layers, 2 done, 1 minimal  penalty 0: g<A>(coercion($y))'. Both derivations (P<C> with the inner fact C ~ A, and P<B> with the outer fact B ~ A) are found, and the penalty rule chooses the one whose equality comes from the closer scope.
FIX and FULL: 1 diagnostic, 'no coercion from 'P<A> ==> [Void]() let -> Void' ...'. The FIX trace shows 'resolve P<A>: 3 layers, 2 done, 2 minimal  penalty 0 / penalty 0'. semtest MISMATCH.
e05-penalty-scope2.hylo (the facts in the other nesting) behaves the same: baseline '2 done, 1 minimal, penalty 1', FIX '2 minimal'.
Related: e01-orient-amb.hylo ('B == A, A == C' with B is P, C is P, g<A>()) is accepted by the baseline and rejected by FIX. That case is only an orientation artifact, because the baseline itself rejects the symmetric e01b ('B == A, C == A'), so FIX is consistent there.

gz1.hylo:
  trait P { type Y }
  trait Q { type O }
  struct T {}
  struct U {}
  given T is P { type Y = U }
  given U is Q { type O = T }
  given Z.O is P { type Y = Int }   // Z.O = U.O = T, so this is a second 'T is P'
  type Z = T.Y
  fun k(z: Z) {}
run.sh output (baseline / FIX / FULL): 0 / 0 / 0 diagnostics. The original baseline 4587ce9e1a23 also gives 0.
gz3.hylo is gz1 with lines 7 and 8 swapped (alias before the given): 1 diagnostic in all three configurations, 'definition of 'Z' refers to itself'.
Controls showing that the program is ambiguous by the checker's own rules:
- red2.hylo: 'given U.O is P' counts as 'T is P'; need<T>() is accepted.
- red3.hylo: that given plus 'given T is P' makes need<T>() fail (ambiguous).
- gz0.hylo: two 'T is P' givens make 'type Z = T.Y' fail with 'ambiguous use of Y'.
- gz1b.hylo is gz1 plus 'fun k3(y: T.Y)' and 'k(U())'. FIX accepts 'type Z = T.Y' as U and k(U()), but in the same program rejects 'y: T.Y' with 'ambiguous use of Y' / 'expression does not denote a type' (2 diagnostics, all configurations).
- gz1c.hylo: need<T>() in the same program is rejected (no coercion, i.e. ambiguous).

ge1.hylo:
  trait P { type Y }
  trait Q { type O }
  struct T {}
  struct U { public memberwise init }
  given T is P { type Y = U }
  given U is Q { type O = T }
  given U.Foo.O is P { type Y = Int }   // = T is P once Foo is known
  extension T.Y { type Foo = U }
  fun k(z: U) {}
Results: baseline 0, FIX 0, FULL 0 diagnostics. The original baseline 4587ce9e1a23 also gives 0.
ge2.hylo is the same program with lines 7 and 8 (the given and the extension) swapped:
- baseline: 1 diagnostic ('type U has no static member Foo');
- FIX and FULL: 2 diagnostics, 'circular extension: the extendee of this extension depends on a lookup of 'Foo'...' and 'type U has no static member Foo'.
Control ge3.hylo (two explicit 'T is P' givens plus 'extension T.Y'): 'ambiguous use of Y' in all configurations.

gx1.hylo:
  trait P {}
  struct I { type Back = A }
  struct A { type Inner = I }
  extension <T where T is P> T { type Inner = Bool }
  given A.Inner.Back is P {}
Results: baseline 0, FIX 0, FULL 0 diagnostics. The original baseline also gives 0.
gx1_use.hylo is the same plus 'fun use(x: A.Inner) {}': 1 diagnostic, 'expression does not denote a type' at A.Inner. That is the very expression the given's head contains.
gx1_ctrl.hylo (with 'given A is P' written directly): the same rejection.

